course: dictionary
lesson: 5004
title: Long words, small meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Inexactitude means not being exact, so a small mistake or error. The word is built from parts: in- means not, and exact means correct. Scientists and maths users say «inexactitude in measurement» when a measurement is slightly off.
explain: Sometimes a long, formal word is used as a joke. «Terminological inexactitude» sounds serious and scientific, but it is a funny, polite way of saying a lie. The big words make the lie sound less rude, and the listener often works it out from the tone or the situation.
explain: Always check the context. Ask: is this sentence about measuring, or about someone not telling the truth? Then swap in your meaning. «The measurement had an inexactitude» means a small error. «That was a terminological inexactitude» means a polite, funny lie.
fact: Inexactitude means being not exact or not correct.
fact: The prefix in- means not, so inexactitude is built from "not" and "exact."
fact: In science and maths, inexactitude means an error when measuring.
fact: «Terminological inexactitude» is a joking, formal euphemism for a lie.
fact: A euphemism is a polite or funny word used in place of a blunt one.
exercise: In a science lab, what does «inexactitude in measurement» mean? | A very long, exact measurement | A happy surprise | A type of ruler | *A small error in measuring
exercise: Someone says, «That was a terminological inexactitude.» What do they really mean? | They are very good at maths | *A polite, funny way of saying a lie | They are measuring something | They are very angry
exercise: Which part of inexactitude means "not"? | *in- | tude | exact | ness
test: The map had an inexactitude, so the distance was slightly wrong. What does inexactitude mean here? | *A small error | A perfectly clear, exact picture | A famous painting | A sudden storm
test: Which phrase is a polite, funny way of saying someone told a lie? | exact answer | precise measurement | *terminological inexactitude | honest reply
exam: In the joke «terminological inexactitude», what does the speaker really mean? | *They are admitting, in a funny way, that what they said was not true | They are saying they are very tired | They are asking for a ruler | They are giving a precise measurement
